00:00:46.313 --> 00:00:47.730
So what we're going
to be covering

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:51.330
is we'll talk about, at a high
level, what extensions are, why

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:55.020
they provide value
in an enterprise,

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:57.060
as well as some
inherent risks that

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:59.160
would be included with
having those present

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:00.930
on your end-users' machines.

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:04.980
And then I'll softball
it over to my pal Nick.

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:08.970
And he'll talk a little bit
more about how Google manages

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:11.610
extensions internally
and how we moved

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:14.550
from a traditional way
of managing extensions,

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:17.400
through whitelists
and blacklists,

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:21.250
to a more enhanced methodology
that will detail there.

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:22.680
And then we'll
wrap everything up.

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:25.950
And we'll talk about how you,
yourselves as administrators,

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:30.010
can be able to roll this
out in your enterprise.

00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:32.350
So let's get started.

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:34.150
So why extensions?

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:35.550
What are extensions?

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:38.580
Obviously-- hopefully-- people
are moderately aware of what

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:40.390
that is, if you're here today.

00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:42.030
But if you're not,
really, it's just

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:44.610
a software program that's
going to customize your Chrome

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:46.440
browser experience.

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:52.590
So users may use it to add
something as simple as a theme,

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:55.920
have a change in how Chrome
browser works from a behavior

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:59.190
perspective-- so every
time I open up a new tab,

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:02.040
I get greeted with a beautiful
kitten picture or something

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:03.150
along those lines--

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:05.820
as well as productivity
tools, where

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:10.470
I need to join a web
meeting or record my screen.

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:12.180
So, really, those are
the values that it

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:16.200
adds from a customization
and productivity side.

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:17.820
But Nick, I'll throw
it over to you.

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:19.320
Tell me a little
bit more about what

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:22.060
risks come with
extensions, especially

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:23.695
at scale in an enterprise.

00:02:23.695 --> 00:02:25.320
NICK PETERSON: Yeah
so while extensions

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:27.030
have a lot of
productivity benefits,

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:28.200
there are some issues.

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:30.540
Because really, you're
extending the browser

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:31.890
and all of its capabilities.

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:34.810
So extensions can
introduce vulnerabilities

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:39.090
if they're not properly coded
or in case issues come up later

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:40.590
with third-party libraries.

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:42.540
Additionally, depending
on the permissions,

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:44.652
they can access your
cookies, which is not good,

00:02:44.652 --> 00:02:46.860
because that's how you
authenticate to your websites.

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:49.140
They can make requests
as you, as well as

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:51.030
inject JavaScript,
which can leak data

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:53.657
if it's not properly coded.

00:02:53.657 --> 00:02:55.240
FLETCHER OLIVER:
Great-- thanks, Nick.

00:02:55.240 --> 00:03:00.910
So some challenges that exist
with managing extensions--

00:03:00.910 --> 00:03:06.250
there's three things that really
make it a painful process.

00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:11.990
First is limited visibility into
what extensions are installed--

00:03:11.990 --> 00:03:15.400
so how they've been installed,
where they're installed,

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:18.130
as well as what
rights or permissions

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:20.110
they require in order to run.

00:03:20.110 --> 00:03:24.060
And then, being able to have
a formal vetting process--

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:26.320
I'll allow this extension.

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:27.670
I won't allow this one.

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:31.270
It can tend to be
confusing-- as well

00:03:31.270 --> 00:03:33.907
as being able to have a
management process around that.

00:03:33.907 --> 00:03:35.740
Especially if you don't
have much visibility

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:37.970
into what extensions
are present,

00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:41.290
it's hard to determine
what you can or can't run,

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:43.840
as they might be
crucial to the end users

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:46.280
in order to get their job done.

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:50.080
So we have a dual
approach to protection.

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:52.300
The first side is
securing the source.

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:54.550
And securing the source--

00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:55.523
that's on us.

00:03:55.523 --> 00:03:57.190
That's on Google to
be able to make sure

00:03:57.190 --> 00:04:01.540
that there is a safe and
secure mechanism for your users

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:05.440
or your administrators to be
able to download and install

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:06.850
extensions.

00:04:06.850 --> 00:04:08.830
On the other hand,
securing the endpoint--

00:04:08.830 --> 00:04:12.980
that's on the administrator,
through different policies.

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:16.690
And we'll talk more about
not only the traditional ways

00:04:16.690 --> 00:04:19.959
of managing extensions but
this enhanced methodology

00:04:19.959 --> 00:04:22.180
that you'll hear us pretty
much blathering around

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:23.690
through the whole session.

00:04:23.690 --> 00:04:26.720
So let's talk about our
side, the Google side,

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:28.550
which is securing the source.

00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:30.910
So we've had some recent
developments in the Chrome Web

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:34.190
Store on enhancing security.

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:36.980
So we've removed
in-line installations.

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:40.120
So extensions either need
to be installed directly

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:44.020
from the Chrome Web Store or
pushed out via Force Install

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:45.610
by an administrator.

00:04:45.610 --> 00:04:48.010
As well as-- we've tightened
the review process.

00:04:48.010 --> 00:04:52.750
So as developers submit their
extensions for approval,

00:04:52.750 --> 00:04:56.140
our algorithms have been
tightened to prevent

00:04:56.140 --> 00:05:00.160
malicious extensions being even
present on the web machines

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:04.190
before they get live or present
on your end-users' machines.

00:05:04.190 --> 00:05:08.380
As well as-- we've developed
and enhanced our platform

00:05:08.380 --> 00:05:12.940
through Manifest Version 3
at a super, super high level.

00:05:12.940 --> 00:05:17.530
We make it easier for
developers to develop extensions

00:05:17.530 --> 00:05:21.010
in a more secure way and
harder for developers

00:05:21.010 --> 00:05:25.340
to create it in a
less secure way.

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:27.680
So policy managements--
this is, as I

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:29.540
said, securing the
end point, which

00:05:29.540 --> 00:05:31.520
is up to the
administrator, in order

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:33.860
to roll out these policies.

00:05:33.860 --> 00:05:36.920
Traditionally, this is all
done through whitelists.

00:05:36.920 --> 00:05:40.670
These extensions are
fine to be installed.

00:05:40.670 --> 00:05:43.400
Blacklists-- these
extensions, I never

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:45.832
want to be installed
on my end-points.

00:05:45.832 --> 00:05:47.540
The problem with
traditional management--

00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:49.940
as we'll talk
throughout the session--

00:05:49.940 --> 00:05:52.610
is those whitelists
and blacklists

00:05:52.610 --> 00:05:55.880
tend to get pretty lengthy.

00:05:55.880 --> 00:05:58.220
And it can be a
full-time job sometimes

00:05:58.220 --> 00:06:00.500
in order to maintain those.

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:03.380
And actually, as Nick
will highlight later

00:06:03.380 --> 00:06:05.510
on in the session,
it's something

00:06:05.510 --> 00:06:07.700
that Google
traditionally, internally,

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:09.890
used to manage through
those traditional whitelists

00:06:09.890 --> 00:06:11.210
and blacklists.

00:06:11.210 --> 00:06:14.670
But we actually moved to
enhanced management style,

00:06:14.670 --> 00:06:16.370
which we'll highlight
and provide you

00:06:16.370 --> 00:06:19.280
guys some tips and tricks on how
you guys can do it yourselves.

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:22.970
But also, it's really
managing by the behavior

00:06:22.970 --> 00:06:25.140
of the extension itself.

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:28.610
So the permissions
or what rights

00:06:28.610 --> 00:06:31.430
those extensions need
in order to run--

00:06:31.430 --> 00:06:34.940
managing just by that, instead
of by the extension itself,

00:06:34.940 --> 00:06:39.020
as well as blocking the action
of the extension on your most

00:06:39.020 --> 00:06:40.400
sensitive websites.

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:44.810
So users are able to get the
extensions that they want.

00:06:44.810 --> 00:06:48.110
But you're able to protect
your most sensitive sites

00:06:48.110 --> 00:06:51.690
from extensions reading
information for them.

00:06:51.690 --> 00:06:57.150
So want I foreshadowed,
I guess, how

00:06:57.150 --> 00:06:59.660
Google moved from a
traditional whitelist

00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:01.893
and blacklist method to
an enhanced methodology.

00:07:01.893 --> 00:07:03.810
So I'm actually going
to turn it over to Nick.

00:07:03.810 --> 00:07:06.570
And he's going to talk about
that journey for Google.

00:07:06.570 --> 00:07:07.430
So go ahead, Nick.

00:07:07.430 --> 00:07:09.510
NICK PETERSON: Thanks, Fletcher.

00:07:09.510 --> 00:07:11.580
So just a little FYI--

00:07:11.580 --> 00:07:15.720
like a lot of other companies,
we have a lot of machines.

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:16.760
I see a lot of machines.

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:18.430
It's in the hundreds
of thousands.

00:07:18.430 --> 00:07:20.730
So trying to make this
work is something--

00:07:20.730 --> 00:07:22.620
we need to make sure it scales.

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:25.620
And rather than whitelisting
one or two extensions,

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:29.580
we have about 60,000 extensions
that our employees actively

00:07:29.580 --> 00:07:30.780
use.

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:32.920
So going through and
individually looking

00:07:32.920 --> 00:07:36.090
at each one of these, isn't
really something we can do.

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:37.680
Additionally, our
employees really

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:40.680
like developing new extensions
for their productivity.

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:43.477
We have around 7,000 extensions.

00:07:43.477 --> 00:07:45.810
And that's not just that
people have made at some point.

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:49.260
But these are actively used and
installed on people's machines.

00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:52.390
So internally, we wanted
to make sure there was

00:07:52.390 --> 00:07:54.210
something that worked for us.

00:07:54.210 --> 00:07:57.930
So the whitelist and
blacklist just wasn't working.

00:07:57.930 --> 00:08:01.260
So our philosophy-- we
could have just said,

00:08:01.260 --> 00:08:04.200
let's just block everything or
let's just allow everything.

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:07.388
But we wanted to make sure
we allowed users to access

00:08:07.388 --> 00:08:08.430
the extensions they need.

00:08:08.430 --> 00:08:10.870
But we also don't want that
additional risk around it.

00:08:10.870 --> 00:08:13.350
So we wanted to have something
that still let people

00:08:13.350 --> 00:08:15.970
be productive and didn't
have a risk to our users,

00:08:15.970 --> 00:08:17.970
because we have the
responsibility of protecting

00:08:17.970 --> 00:08:19.920
the data that our
users entrust with us.

00:08:23.320 --> 00:08:24.945
So as Fletcher
mentioned earlier,

00:08:24.945 --> 00:08:27.320
when we're using the traditional
blacklist and whitelist,

00:08:27.320 --> 00:08:29.830
it was missing the mark.

00:08:29.830 --> 00:08:31.750
There was limitations.

00:08:31.750 --> 00:08:35.289
Our blacklists we're getting
into the tens of thousands

00:08:35.289 --> 00:08:36.970
of extensions long.

00:08:36.970 --> 00:08:39.100
And that's just not
something that's doable.

00:08:39.100 --> 00:08:42.610
That also means that reviewing
these tens of thousands

00:08:42.610 --> 00:08:44.920
of extensions on the whitelist
isn't really something

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:46.750
we can do in depth.

00:08:46.750 --> 00:08:49.180
And it really doesn't
help the situation

00:08:49.180 --> 00:08:51.880
when an extension
can go and pull code

00:08:51.880 --> 00:08:54.400
from a different website
the moment it loads,

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:58.110
because there's really
no way to review that.

00:08:58.110 --> 00:09:01.243
Additionally, reviewing
these means you need someone

00:09:01.243 --> 00:09:02.910
to be able to go
through the JavaScript,

00:09:02.910 --> 00:09:05.070
figure out what it's doing.

00:09:05.070 --> 00:09:06.540
And when it's
minified and things

00:09:06.540 --> 00:09:08.890
like, that it makes
it really hard to do.

00:09:08.890 --> 00:09:12.088
So it wasn't really giving
us the security we needed.

00:09:12.088 --> 00:09:14.130
So we needed to come up
with something different.

00:09:14.130 --> 00:09:17.533
We needed something that
would balance the productivity

00:09:17.533 --> 00:09:19.950
of the users-- so they got to
use extensions that wanted--

00:09:19.950 --> 00:09:21.992
so they weren't waiting
on to review an extension

00:09:21.992 --> 00:09:23.070
and whitelist it--

00:09:23.070 --> 00:09:24.930
along with the
needs of security,

00:09:24.930 --> 00:09:28.740
because we need to protect our
employees as well as the data

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:32.630
that our users entrust with us.

00:09:32.630 --> 00:09:35.960
So we have a new approach that
Fletcher talked about earlier.

00:09:35.960 --> 00:09:39.920
It's enhanced
management capability.

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:42.560
So we needed something to scale,
to fit those tens of thousands

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:45.110
of extensions, that increased
user productivity, so they're

00:09:45.110 --> 00:09:47.150
not waiting on us to
whitelist extensions,

00:09:47.150 --> 00:09:50.130
as well as mitigating the
risk that came along with it.

00:09:50.130 --> 00:09:52.400
And we did this through
the introduction

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:54.518
of managed by permission
and the runtime host

00:09:54.518 --> 00:09:56.560
controls, which we'll talk
about in a little bit.

00:10:02.120 --> 00:10:05.060
Before we talk about
managing by permission,

00:10:05.060 --> 00:10:07.760
we need to understand what
permissions really are.

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:10.617
So to limit the risk
to users, extensions

00:10:10.617 --> 00:10:12.200
must declare which
sites they're going

00:10:12.200 --> 00:10:16.100
to interact with as well as the
risky actions they may take.

00:10:16.100 --> 00:10:18.480
And they do this by
requiring developers,

00:10:18.480 --> 00:10:20.060
when they're making
the extension,

00:10:20.060 --> 00:10:22.612
to declare permissions.

00:10:22.612 --> 00:10:24.320
And depending on what
the extension does,

00:10:24.320 --> 00:10:27.470
some sites have say permissions,
some have device permissions.

00:10:27.470 --> 00:10:28.838
Some have both.

00:10:28.838 --> 00:10:30.380
And it's the
combination of these two

00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:32.060
that cover most of
the risky actions

00:10:32.060 --> 00:10:33.860
that an extension can take.

00:10:33.860 --> 00:10:35.810
So the site permissions
are the list

00:10:35.810 --> 00:10:38.240
of sites that an extension
may interact with.

00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:41.210
And the device permissions
cover the risky actions

00:10:41.210 --> 00:10:43.100
specific to the device.

00:10:43.100 --> 00:10:46.100
And the convergence of these
two cover most of the risk.

00:10:49.180 --> 00:10:51.660
So when we talk about
manage by permission,

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:53.670
what this is actually
allowing you to do

00:10:53.670 --> 00:10:57.570
is block the installation
of an extension,

00:10:57.570 --> 00:11:00.150
specifically by the
permissions it declares.

00:11:00.150 --> 00:11:03.480
So if you don't
want an extension

00:11:03.480 --> 00:11:06.800
to be able to view the
screen, you could say,

00:11:06.800 --> 00:11:09.090
the Desktop Capture
permission is blocked.

00:11:09.090 --> 00:11:10.920
And then you
wouldn't have to look

00:11:10.920 --> 00:11:12.690
at the code of each extension.

00:11:12.690 --> 00:11:14.640
Chrome would know,
at installation time,

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:18.558
that extension has a
Desktop Capture permission.

00:11:18.558 --> 00:11:20.350
Additionally, you can
configure extensions.

00:11:20.350 --> 00:11:24.630
So if there is a extension
that you want to allow,

00:11:24.630 --> 00:11:27.600
even though it has pretty good
permission, you can do that.

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:30.150
This also allows you to-- what
we call "permission pinning."

00:11:30.150 --> 00:11:32.220
So if you have an
extension you like,

00:11:32.220 --> 00:11:33.910
you can list the
permissions it can use.

00:11:33.910 --> 00:11:36.780
And if in the future it updates
and adds a new permission,

00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:39.780
it's instantly disabled
on all of your users,

00:11:39.780 --> 00:11:44.400
until you modify it the policy.

00:11:44.400 --> 00:11:46.200
So you don't have to
worry about extensions

00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:48.420
updating new permissions
without you knowing.

00:11:48.420 --> 00:11:51.360
And, additionally, you can list
a custom installation error

00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:52.240
message.

00:11:52.240 --> 00:11:54.073
So when users are
blocked, you can tell them

00:11:54.073 --> 00:11:55.145
where to go to get help.

00:11:58.330 --> 00:12:02.533
So this new method is
allowing you to be safer

00:12:02.533 --> 00:12:03.700
from compromised extensions.

00:12:03.700 --> 00:12:05.500
Because rather than
worrying about what

00:12:05.500 --> 00:12:07.810
happens if this thing
updates or making sure

00:12:07.810 --> 00:12:09.670
that you've reviewed
the extension properly,

00:12:09.670 --> 00:12:11.158
you're looking at its behavior.

00:12:11.158 --> 00:12:13.450
And you don't have these long
whitelists and blacklists

00:12:13.450 --> 00:12:13.950
anymore.

00:12:13.950 --> 00:12:15.670
You don't have to
worry about updates.

00:12:15.670 --> 00:12:19.130
And you don't have to vet
each extension individually.

00:12:19.130 --> 00:12:21.900
So let's take a look at
how this looks in action.

00:12:21.900 --> 00:12:24.883
FLETCHER OLIVER:
Great-- all right.

00:12:24.883 --> 00:12:25.550
So thanks, Nick.

00:12:25.550 --> 00:12:27.870
Yeah-- so we talked about
block by permission.

00:12:27.870 --> 00:12:32.730
We talked about how it works
and the value that it offers.

00:12:32.730 --> 00:12:35.250
But let's see it in action, OK?

00:12:35.250 --> 00:12:37.600
So, in this scenario,
I have a user.

00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:40.980
And that user has customized
their browser experience.

00:12:40.980 --> 00:12:43.830
They've installed this
extension that, every time they

00:12:43.830 --> 00:12:45.720
open a new tab,
they get presented

00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:48.690
with a super, awesome,
anime-style studio

00:12:48.690 --> 00:12:50.260
Ghibli screenshot.

00:12:50.260 --> 00:12:50.910
It's dynamic.

00:12:50.910 --> 00:12:52.350
It looks great.

00:12:52.350 --> 00:12:54.210
Myself, as an administrator--

00:12:54.210 --> 00:12:56.250
really don't care
if they have this

00:12:56.250 --> 00:12:57.750
installed on their machine.

00:12:57.750 --> 00:13:01.380
If they want to anime it up,
I'm totally cool with it.

00:13:01.380 --> 00:13:04.420
However-- and unlucky
for this user--

00:13:04.420 --> 00:13:07.110
this particular extension
uses a permission

00:13:07.110 --> 00:13:12.270
or has a specific right that
I have determined is no bueno.

00:13:12.270 --> 00:13:18.210
So this is a screenshot of the
manifest for this extension.

00:13:18.210 --> 00:13:23.070
And I've highlighted,
in my research,

00:13:23.070 --> 00:13:25.650
that there's a certain
permission that I say,

00:13:25.650 --> 00:13:29.500
no way is this going to
run on any of my devices.

00:13:29.500 --> 00:13:32.970
And that permission is
the Storage permission.

00:13:32.970 --> 00:13:35.160
Quick side note-- there's
not really much wrong

00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:36.420
with the Storage permission.

00:13:36.420 --> 00:13:37.920
It's not nefarious or
anything like that.

00:13:37.920 --> 00:13:39.300
Just for demonstration
purposes--

00:13:39.300 --> 00:13:40.230
I picked it.

00:13:40.230 --> 00:13:42.480
So unfortunately
for my user, they're

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:45.000
going to lose their super,
awesome, studio Ghibli anime

00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:46.050
theme.

00:13:46.050 --> 00:13:47.820
But lucky for me,
we're going to be

00:13:47.820 --> 00:13:51.160
able to secure the end-point,
which is really the goal.

00:13:51.160 --> 00:13:53.130
So there's actually
three ways that you can

00:13:53.130 --> 00:13:56.490
be able to do this via policy.

00:13:56.490 --> 00:14:00.150
So the first way
is Group Policy.

00:14:00.150 --> 00:14:03.690
And this is the way
that most enterprises

00:14:03.690 --> 00:14:05.010
are managing Chrome Browser.

00:14:05.010 --> 00:14:09.090
So if you're not
managing via Group Policy

00:14:09.090 --> 00:14:11.070
and you have it in
your environment,

00:14:11.070 --> 00:14:14.310
I highly recommend
that you take a look

00:14:14.310 --> 00:14:16.800
and download the Chrome
Enterprise bundle.

00:14:16.800 --> 00:14:18.990
It includes some
handy-dandy MSIs

00:14:18.990 --> 00:14:21.870
that you can use to deploy
out to your end-users

00:14:21.870 --> 00:14:25.570
through your application
deployment method of choice,

00:14:25.570 --> 00:14:28.260
as well as 300-plus
policies that you

00:14:28.260 --> 00:14:33.600
can use to maintain
the look and feel,

00:14:33.600 --> 00:14:37.780
manage bookmarks, security
settings-- all that stuff.

00:14:37.780 --> 00:14:40.110
So if you have the
super, super eagle eyes,

00:14:40.110 --> 00:14:43.740
you can see that I'm in the
Extension Management Settings

00:14:43.740 --> 00:14:45.590
policy.

00:14:45.590 --> 00:14:46.950
But I'll zoom in for you.

00:14:46.950 --> 00:14:51.590
So I put a JSON string in there.

00:14:51.590 --> 00:14:53.460
And if you take a
look at the left--

00:14:53.460 --> 00:14:55.730
or stage left, right-left--

00:14:55.730 --> 00:14:56.540
I don't know.

00:14:56.540 --> 00:14:57.650
There's a star.

00:14:57.650 --> 00:15:01.220
And in the star, that denotes
that this is a default policy.

00:15:01.220 --> 00:15:04.100
And what that means
is that this policy

00:15:04.100 --> 00:15:07.010
will apply to all extensions.

00:15:07.010 --> 00:15:10.490
So extensions that are already
installed will be disabled.

00:15:10.490 --> 00:15:13.640
And any extensions that
are going to be installed

00:15:13.640 --> 00:15:14.930
will be blocked.

00:15:14.930 --> 00:15:18.933
Just note that, if the
extension's already present,

00:15:18.933 --> 00:15:19.850
it'll just disable it.

00:15:19.850 --> 00:15:21.700
It will not uninstall it.

00:15:21.700 --> 00:15:24.950
And then I've declared
that the actual blocked

00:15:24.950 --> 00:15:28.070
permission-- and then
Storage is the permission

00:15:28.070 --> 00:15:29.270
that I'm blocking.

00:15:29.270 --> 00:15:31.610
So I talked about--
there's three ways.

00:15:31.610 --> 00:15:33.060
First way-- Group Policy.

00:15:33.060 --> 00:15:35.760
Second-- Windows Registry.

00:15:35.760 --> 00:15:40.100
So, if you don't necessarily
like to write JSON,

00:15:40.100 --> 00:15:42.778
which maybe there's some
JSON gurus in the audience.

00:15:42.778 --> 00:15:43.320
I'm not sure.

00:15:43.320 --> 00:15:46.650
I'm sure you can get your
kicks on the Group Policy part.

00:15:46.650 --> 00:15:54.230
But here is the location where
you can create a sub-key under

00:15:54.230 --> 00:16:00.735
HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE SOFTWARE
Policy Google Chrome--

00:16:00.735 --> 00:16:01.610
yeah, I got them all.

00:16:01.610 --> 00:16:02.900
And then the Extension Settings.

00:16:02.900 --> 00:16:04.525
And then there's a
sub-key under there.

00:16:04.525 --> 00:16:05.520
See that star again?

00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:06.737
So this is a default policy.

00:16:06.737 --> 00:16:08.570
And before you start
pulling out your phones

00:16:08.570 --> 00:16:10.510
and taking photos or--

00:16:10.510 --> 00:16:12.530
no, you're totally fine.

00:16:12.530 --> 00:16:14.833
I was going to say that we
actually have a white paper

00:16:14.833 --> 00:16:17.000
that's coming out-- it's
called "Managing Extensions

00:16:17.000 --> 00:16:19.970
in your Enterprise"-- that
has step-by-step instructions.

00:16:19.970 --> 00:16:23.480
But you can feel free to take
your photo whenever you want.

00:16:23.480 --> 00:16:24.680
It's not NDA or anything.

00:16:24.680 --> 00:16:26.510
Otherwise, there would have
been a million slides that say,

00:16:26.510 --> 00:16:27.530
do not take photos!

00:16:27.530 --> 00:16:30.530
Anyway, we have a white
paper that's coming out,

00:16:30.530 --> 00:16:32.300
which has step-by-step
processes not

00:16:32.300 --> 00:16:38.407
only to do this enhanced method
but also the traditional way.

00:16:38.407 --> 00:16:40.490
So maybe the enhanced
method isn't right for you--

00:16:40.490 --> 00:16:41.660
people like options.

00:16:41.660 --> 00:16:43.410
So if you want traditional
whitelists and blacklist,

00:16:43.410 --> 00:16:45.618
it has step-by-step instructions
in order to do that.

00:16:45.618 --> 00:16:47.850
And that's actually was
published yesterday.

00:16:47.850 --> 00:16:50.540
So at the end, we'll
have some resource links.

00:16:50.540 --> 00:16:52.673
And in a couple of
days or so, it'll

00:16:52.673 --> 00:16:53.840
get pushed to your next app.

00:16:53.840 --> 00:16:55.940
You can go ahead
and click on that.

00:16:55.940 --> 00:16:57.050
So I've created this key.

00:16:57.050 --> 00:17:00.800
It actually converts it to JSON
on the back end, which is nice.

00:17:00.800 --> 00:17:02.720
And I talked about three ways--

00:17:02.720 --> 00:17:05.420
Group Policy, Windows Registry.

00:17:05.420 --> 00:17:09.230
And the last one is the
Google Admin Console

00:17:09.230 --> 00:17:12.829
or the new Chrome Browser
Cloud Management feature

00:17:12.829 --> 00:17:14.648
that was just released--

00:17:14.648 --> 00:17:15.440
Phil, was it today?

00:17:15.440 --> 00:17:16.099
PHIL: Yes.

00:17:16.099 --> 00:17:18.970
FLETCHER OLIVER: It was
today, so hurray for you guys

00:17:18.970 --> 00:17:19.819
and for us.

00:17:19.819 --> 00:17:21.920
So Chrome Browser
Cloud Management--

00:17:21.920 --> 00:17:24.740
if you guys haven't heard of it,
it's an exciting new feature.

00:17:24.740 --> 00:17:25.940
It's a free feature.

00:17:25.940 --> 00:17:30.350
And it's a great way to not
only be able to push policies

00:17:30.350 --> 00:17:34.640
from the cloud but, also,
gives you really enhanced view

00:17:34.640 --> 00:17:37.190
into the extensions that
are installed and present

00:17:37.190 --> 00:17:39.230
on your users' machines.

00:17:39.230 --> 00:17:41.330
As I talked way, way long
ago, in the beginning

00:17:41.330 --> 00:17:44.390
of the presentation,
that's the pain, right?--

00:17:44.390 --> 00:17:47.150
being able to find out what
extensions are installed, how

00:17:47.150 --> 00:17:49.640
they were installed, and
what permissions or rights

00:17:49.640 --> 00:17:51.683
they require in order to run.

00:17:51.683 --> 00:17:54.350
So you can actually get all that
information from Chrome Browser

00:17:54.350 --> 00:17:55.058
Cloud Management.

00:17:55.058 --> 00:17:56.930
So I highly recommend
you take a look at it.

00:17:56.930 --> 00:18:01.170
And if you wanted to do
this blocked by permission,

00:18:01.170 --> 00:18:02.700
you check a box.

00:18:02.700 --> 00:18:05.760
So like I said, not throwing
anything up on the JSON people

00:18:05.760 --> 00:18:07.887
that like to write JSON,
but checking a box, to me,

00:18:07.887 --> 00:18:08.970
seems a little bit easier.

00:18:08.970 --> 00:18:10.803
At least, it was easy
to get the screenshot.

00:18:10.803 --> 00:18:17.850
So I've rolled this out through
one of my three policies--

00:18:17.850 --> 00:18:22.200
Group Policy, Windows Registry,
and the Google Admin Console

00:18:22.200 --> 00:18:25.050
or the new Chrome
Browser Cloud Management.

00:18:25.050 --> 00:18:26.680
And let's see what happens.

00:18:26.680 --> 00:18:29.160
So sad face for my user--

00:18:29.160 --> 00:18:30.510
I've updated the policy.

00:18:30.510 --> 00:18:31.470
And they get a message.

00:18:31.470 --> 00:18:33.840
And it says, this
has been blocked.

00:18:33.840 --> 00:18:37.290
As Nick highlighted before,
you can customize this message

00:18:37.290 --> 00:18:41.402
to say, Contact IT, File a
ticket through your ITSM tool--

00:18:41.402 --> 00:18:42.360
whatever you guys like.

00:18:42.360 --> 00:18:45.650
So you can see, once it
blocks, I open up new tabs.

00:18:45.650 --> 00:18:50.940
And unfortunately for them,
the anime wallpaper is gone.

00:18:50.940 --> 00:18:52.430
So poor one out for this user.

00:18:52.430 --> 00:18:53.013
So all right--

00:18:53.013 --> 00:18:54.388
NICK PETERSON:
And it also blocks

00:18:54.388 --> 00:18:56.190
the installation
of new extensions,

00:18:56.190 --> 00:18:58.448
not just the currently
installed extensions as well.

00:18:58.448 --> 00:18:59.490
FLETCHER OLIVER: Exactly.

00:18:59.490 --> 00:19:00.930
So Nick, tell us
a little bit more

00:19:00.930 --> 00:19:02.670
about runtime host controls.

00:19:02.670 --> 00:19:05.640
NICK PETERSON: Sure-- so blocked
by permission is helpful.

00:19:05.640 --> 00:19:07.260
But it's a rather
heavy-handed way

00:19:07.260 --> 00:19:10.200
of going about things
sometimes, because that

00:19:10.200 --> 00:19:13.450
means the user can't
actually use the extension.

00:19:13.450 --> 00:19:16.410
So another option is
Runtime Host Controls.

00:19:16.410 --> 00:19:18.480
And what this feature
does is it lets

00:19:18.480 --> 00:19:22.560
you define a list of websites
that you consider sensitive.

00:19:22.560 --> 00:19:25.860
And it lets the extension
work on all of the sites

00:19:25.860 --> 00:19:28.210
except for the ones you
define as sensitive.

00:19:28.210 --> 00:19:29.700
So when they start
to do anything

00:19:29.700 --> 00:19:33.090
like reading cookies or
injecting JavaScripts

00:19:33.090 --> 00:19:35.830
or modifying web requests
about a particular site,

00:19:35.830 --> 00:19:37.770
the browser will say,
what site are you

00:19:37.770 --> 00:19:38.880
attempting to do this on?

00:19:38.880 --> 00:19:40.650
And if it's one of the
listed sensitive sites,

00:19:40.650 --> 00:19:41.650
then it will be blocked.

00:19:41.650 --> 00:19:44.200
So the users still get to
install their extensions.

00:19:44.200 --> 00:19:47.190
And it'll work on all the
other websites, just not

00:19:47.190 --> 00:19:49.230
your websites which have
your sensitive customer

00:19:49.230 --> 00:19:52.220
data on them.

00:19:52.220 --> 00:19:56.170
So the benefits-- the users
can use the more extensions.

00:19:56.170 --> 00:19:58.865
And you also get to protect
your most sensitive data.

00:19:58.865 --> 00:20:00.240
You still don't
have to do anyway

00:20:00.240 --> 00:20:01.282
whitelists or blacklists.

00:20:01.282 --> 00:20:02.890
You don't have to
review any updates.

00:20:02.890 --> 00:20:05.320
And you don't have to look at
the code of each extension.

00:20:05.320 --> 00:20:07.822
You simply defined the list
of sites you want to protect.

00:20:07.822 --> 00:20:08.780
FLETCHER OLIVER: Great.

00:20:08.780 --> 00:20:10.738
NICK PETERSON: And the
combination of these two

00:20:10.738 --> 00:20:12.600
is really what we
rely on, instead

00:20:12.600 --> 00:20:14.130
of the whitelist and blacklist.

00:20:14.130 --> 00:20:16.200
FLETCHER OLIVER: So that's
what Google IT uses internally.

00:20:16.200 --> 00:20:17.158
NICK PETERSON: Exactly.

00:20:17.158 --> 00:20:18.930
FLETCHER OLIVER: Great.

00:20:18.930 --> 00:20:20.790
All right-- so let's
see it in action.

00:20:20.790 --> 00:20:23.410
So I have a user.

00:20:23.410 --> 00:20:24.870
They have an
extension installed.

00:20:24.870 --> 00:20:29.523
This extension toggles a
super cool and edgy dark mode.

00:20:29.523 --> 00:20:30.440
They can toggle it on.

00:20:30.440 --> 00:20:32.570
They can toggle it off.

00:20:32.570 --> 00:20:37.670
And as I felt the same way
about my anime wallpaper user,

00:20:37.670 --> 00:20:40.680
I feel the exact same way
about my dark mode user.

00:20:40.680 --> 00:20:43.760
I do not care if you have
this extension installed.

00:20:43.760 --> 00:20:46.995
I have other things
to worry about.

00:20:46.995 --> 00:20:49.370
So yes, if they want to ruin
their eyes in their parents'

00:20:49.370 --> 00:20:52.010
basement-- totally fine with me.

00:20:52.010 --> 00:20:55.820
However, I've denoted that
Google Arts and Culture

00:20:55.820 --> 00:20:58.250
is my sensitive site.

00:20:58.250 --> 00:21:02.780
And I don't want any
extension to read any data off

00:21:02.780 --> 00:21:06.320
of my sensitive site,
because it could have my IP.

00:21:06.320 --> 00:21:09.530
It could have PII or Personal
Identifying Information

00:21:09.530 --> 00:21:12.550
for my users or my customers.

00:21:12.550 --> 00:21:14.990
So no extensions can
run on that site.

00:21:14.990 --> 00:21:18.560
So let's talk about
how you can do it.

00:21:18.560 --> 00:21:20.590
So we talked about three ways.

00:21:20.590 --> 00:21:23.620
As you guessed, the first
one is Group Policy.

00:21:23.620 --> 00:21:27.460
I'm back in my friendly
Nest, which is the extension

00:21:27.460 --> 00:21:29.290
management settings policy.

00:21:29.290 --> 00:21:31.510
And I have some JSON.

00:21:31.510 --> 00:21:33.590
I'm using the star for
the default policy.

00:21:33.590 --> 00:21:35.470
So this is going to
say, no extensions

00:21:35.470 --> 00:21:40.060
can run on my super
secret public site

00:21:40.060 --> 00:21:42.070
ArtsandCulture.Google.com.

00:21:42.070 --> 00:21:45.190
As you could see, there's
some stars also in the URL.

00:21:45.190 --> 00:21:47.860
So there's some syntax that you
can put in here to make sure

00:21:47.860 --> 00:21:51.378
that you can-- this will
cover, like HTTP, HTTPS--

00:21:51.378 --> 00:21:53.170
as well as you can put
some stars in there.

00:21:53.170 --> 00:21:54.820
So that way, you
cover your sub pages.

00:21:54.820 --> 00:21:57.460
You're not just covering
your landing page.

00:21:57.460 --> 00:21:59.420
So first way-- Group Policy.

00:21:59.420 --> 00:22:01.508
Second way-- Windows Registry.

00:22:01.508 --> 00:22:03.550
So you can see, I'm kind
of a broken record here.

00:22:03.550 --> 00:22:05.860
So it's the same sub-key.

00:22:05.860 --> 00:22:08.920
And I've just added
a string there

00:22:08.920 --> 00:22:10.930
for runtime_blocked_hosts.

00:22:10.930 --> 00:22:12.730
And that value is the same way.

00:22:12.730 --> 00:22:15.510
So Group Policy--

00:22:15.510 --> 00:22:17.100
Windows Registry.

00:22:17.100 --> 00:22:20.970
Big surprise-- last one is the
Admin Console or Chrome Browser

00:22:20.970 --> 00:22:22.380
Cloud Management.

00:22:22.380 --> 00:22:25.020
So I'm actually in
the same setting

00:22:25.020 --> 00:22:27.120
where I did the
block by permission.

00:22:27.120 --> 00:22:29.490
Just right under there where
I checked storage before,

00:22:29.490 --> 00:22:31.680
I have a blocked URL section.

00:22:31.680 --> 00:22:33.307
So I just put that
syntax in there.

00:22:33.307 --> 00:22:34.890
And if I want to
block multiple sites,

00:22:34.890 --> 00:22:37.240
I just hit Enter, put
one under the other.

00:22:37.240 --> 00:22:40.410
And then I can block my
most sensitive sites.

00:22:40.410 --> 00:22:45.130
So Group Policy, Windows
Registry, Google Admin Console,

00:22:45.130 --> 00:22:46.660
Chrome Browser
Cloud Management--

00:22:46.660 --> 00:22:48.460
your flavor of choice.

00:22:48.460 --> 00:22:51.440
And let's see the after effects.

00:22:51.440 --> 00:22:52.510
So the user goes.

00:22:52.510 --> 00:22:53.530
The policy is applied.

00:22:53.530 --> 00:22:57.940
He goes to-- he or she or they--

00:22:57.940 --> 00:22:59.350
click on the Toggle.

00:22:59.350 --> 00:23:02.020
And it does not affect
my hypersensitive site

00:23:02.020 --> 00:23:03.730
of Google Arts and Culture.

00:23:03.730 --> 00:23:08.950
However, it's able to work
just fine on CNN.travel.

00:23:08.950 --> 00:23:13.990
So this is really what we're
trying to get to-- what every

00:23:13.990 --> 00:23:18.250
IT administrator's
trying to get to--

00:23:18.250 --> 00:23:22.300
that nirvana state that
people don't think exists.

00:23:22.300 --> 00:23:24.280
But it's the balance
between users

00:23:24.280 --> 00:23:26.770
actually getting what
they want and being

00:23:26.770 --> 00:23:28.820
secure on the endpoint.

00:23:28.820 --> 00:23:31.360
So users-- they can still
install these extensions.

00:23:31.360 --> 00:23:34.720
That can still run them and
get the customized experience

00:23:34.720 --> 00:23:37.190
or those productivity tools.

00:23:37.190 --> 00:23:39.700
But also, we have a
dual layer of security

00:23:39.700 --> 00:23:42.400
to make sure that users--

00:23:42.400 --> 00:23:45.250
we're protecting our
most sensitive sites

00:23:45.250 --> 00:23:48.250
and our endpoints
at the same time.

00:23:48.250 --> 00:23:50.160
So Nick--

00:23:50.160 --> 00:23:52.660
NICK PETERSON: Yeah, so when
we went about rolling this out,

00:23:52.660 --> 00:23:54.400
the first step was
really discovering

00:23:54.400 --> 00:23:56.200
what extensions are out there.

00:23:56.200 --> 00:23:58.390
And we needed to be
able to determine

00:23:58.390 --> 00:23:59.890
what permissions they use.

00:23:59.890 --> 00:24:02.050
So we didn't simply
push out a change

00:24:02.050 --> 00:24:04.780
and affect everyone and
remove all their extensions.

00:24:04.780 --> 00:24:06.610
We needed to know
what the impact was.

00:24:06.610 --> 00:24:09.340
So this is something that
external customers can also

00:24:09.340 --> 00:24:12.758
use CBCM for, the Chrome
Browser Cloud Management,

00:24:12.758 --> 00:24:14.800
to be able to determine
what extensions you have,

00:24:14.800 --> 00:24:17.103
as well as what
permissions are on those.

00:24:17.103 --> 00:24:18.520
And from there,
you can figure out

00:24:18.520 --> 00:24:20.650
which permissions you're
going to want to block,

00:24:20.650 --> 00:24:23.223
as well as which extensions
need an exception.

00:24:23.223 --> 00:24:24.640
So the next step
was the planning.

00:24:24.640 --> 00:24:27.170
That was the grandfathering
in the existing extinctions.

00:24:27.170 --> 00:24:28.870
So our users didn't notice.

00:24:28.870 --> 00:24:31.570
So we grandfathered in all
of our existing extensions

00:24:31.570 --> 00:24:33.260
to just stop the bleeding.

00:24:33.260 --> 00:24:36.370
And then we'd go from there and
whittle that whitelist down.

00:24:36.370 --> 00:24:39.340
So we audited that whitelist and
removed the unnecessary entries

00:24:39.340 --> 00:24:40.870
as time went on.

00:24:40.870 --> 00:24:43.540
After that, we did that
permission-based blocking.

00:24:43.540 --> 00:24:47.350
Then we went back and we did
the runtime host policies.

00:24:47.350 --> 00:24:49.640
And we added those
in one by one,

00:24:49.640 --> 00:24:52.540
just slowly making sure
that we didn't affect users.

00:24:55.080 --> 00:24:58.740
And from then on, for
ongoing maintenance,

00:24:58.740 --> 00:25:00.240
we created,
essentially, a flowchart

00:25:00.240 --> 00:25:03.150
that we handed to our Help
Desk that they can simply

00:25:03.150 --> 00:25:05.790
follow-- whether or not to add
an extension to the whitelist,

00:25:05.790 --> 00:25:08.520
based on what it's doing,
what existing things are

00:25:08.520 --> 00:25:09.660
whitelisted.

00:25:09.660 --> 00:25:12.080
And from that, we actually
have a fairly low queue.

00:25:12.080 --> 00:25:17.340
It's about, I think, about a
1/4 of an FTE currently that is

00:25:17.340 --> 00:25:18.840
required to run that-- or less--

00:25:18.840 --> 00:25:20.200
for a company our size.

00:25:20.200 --> 00:25:23.160
So it's definitely something
they can scale pretty well.

00:25:23.160 --> 00:25:27.570
FLETCHER OLIVER: Great,
what was the before state?

00:25:27.570 --> 00:25:29.910
Obviously, the blacklists and
whitelist was like "rrrr."

00:25:29.910 --> 00:25:31.530
NICK PETERSON: Yeah--
many tens of thousands

00:25:31.530 --> 00:25:32.670
of extensions on our blacklist.

00:25:32.670 --> 00:25:34.378
And even then, it
wasn't really giving us

00:25:34.378 --> 00:25:36.270
the security benefits we needed.

00:25:36.270 --> 00:25:39.270
FLETCHER OLIVER: OK, and I would
assume that you're this laissez

00:25:39.270 --> 00:25:41.675
faire person, like leaning
back and just kicking it.

00:25:41.675 --> 00:25:43.800
NICK PETERSON: Oh, it's
certainly a lot better now.

00:25:43.800 --> 00:25:44.790
FLETCHER OLIVER: You
seem relaxed today.

00:25:44.790 --> 00:25:45.360
So that's good.

00:25:45.360 --> 00:25:46.510
NICK PETERSON: Yeah, yeah--

00:25:46.510 --> 00:25:47.363
this has helped.

00:25:47.363 --> 00:25:49.030
FLETCHER OLIVER: Look
at us with banter.

00:25:49.030 --> 00:25:49.613
This is great.

00:25:49.613 --> 00:25:54.900
OK, so how do you guys apply
this in your enterprise?

00:25:54.900 --> 00:25:55.890
Pretty similar-- right?

00:25:55.890 --> 00:25:57.420
And gosh!-- I'm just
going to plug it again--

00:25:57.420 --> 00:25:59.040
Chrome Browser
Cloud Management--

00:25:59.040 --> 00:26:01.500
being able to get visibility
into the extensions that

00:26:01.500 --> 00:26:04.823
are installed-- it's
really the first step.

00:26:04.823 --> 00:26:06.240
You need to find
out where they're

00:26:06.240 --> 00:26:10.230
installed, who installed them,
what permissions they require.

00:26:10.230 --> 00:26:12.980
And you can really get all this
visibility in Chrome Browser

00:26:12.980 --> 00:26:14.510
Cloud Management.

00:26:14.510 --> 00:26:17.400
And it is the
first step in order

00:26:17.400 --> 00:26:20.610
to get to enhanced
extension management--

00:26:20.610 --> 00:26:22.320
is auditing the extensions.

00:26:22.320 --> 00:26:24.210
Because if you don't
know what's out there

00:26:24.210 --> 00:26:28.590
and what different departments
or geolocations are using,

00:26:28.590 --> 00:26:30.480
there could be some
issues when you just

00:26:30.480 --> 00:26:34.200
start rolling out policies that
may block extensions that are

00:26:34.200 --> 00:26:37.170
crucial to core applications.

00:26:37.170 --> 00:26:39.550
So whether you use Chrome
Browser Cloud Management--

00:26:39.550 --> 00:26:43.950
which obviously, I'm biased to
recommend, but I recommend--

00:26:43.950 --> 00:26:45.430
you can also survey
your end users.

00:26:45.430 --> 00:26:48.510
So call, email--
however you do it--

00:26:48.510 --> 00:26:53.130
pester them, until they find
those gold users that represent

00:26:53.130 --> 00:26:55.290
each individual
department or geolocation

00:26:55.290 --> 00:26:57.840
and find out what extensions
that have installed.

00:26:57.840 --> 00:27:00.630
And then take that information
to determine and build

00:27:00.630 --> 00:27:01.980
a master list.

00:27:01.980 --> 00:27:03.600
So this would be--

00:27:03.600 --> 00:27:06.870
these are the hosts that
I know must be secure--

00:27:06.870 --> 00:27:07.990
that I need to protect.

00:27:07.990 --> 00:27:11.290
So those would be the runtime
host ones that you would block.

00:27:11.290 --> 00:27:12.248
Yeah-- exactly.

00:27:12.248 --> 00:27:13.290
I'm agreeing with myself.

00:27:13.290 --> 00:27:14.660
Sorry.

00:27:14.660 --> 00:27:16.650
And then these are the
permissions to restrict.

00:27:16.650 --> 00:27:18.233
These are the
permissions I never want

00:27:18.233 --> 00:27:22.020
to run on any of my endpoints.

00:27:22.020 --> 00:27:24.240
And then review any
existing process.

00:27:24.240 --> 00:27:27.780
And as Nick
highlighted, you might

00:27:27.780 --> 00:27:29.490
find that you're
whitelist and blacklist

00:27:29.490 --> 00:27:31.500
might be able to be shortened.

00:27:31.500 --> 00:27:34.260
And you might be able to
let some extensions run

00:27:34.260 --> 00:27:36.600
and be installed that you
normally wouldn't have been

00:27:36.600 --> 00:27:39.840
able to let go in the wild.

00:27:39.840 --> 00:27:41.670
And then the rest of
these steps are really

00:27:41.670 --> 00:27:45.180
just traditional
change management,

00:27:45.180 --> 00:27:46.377
IT project management.

00:27:46.377 --> 00:27:48.210
You're going to want
to talk to the people--

00:27:48.210 --> 00:27:49.620
the department heads.

00:27:49.620 --> 00:27:51.760
Because you're going to
be affecting their users.

00:27:51.760 --> 00:27:54.240
And you're going to want to
test it in a lab and a pilot.

00:27:54.240 --> 00:27:57.270
I know, nobody in this room
tests this in production,

00:27:57.270 --> 00:27:58.035
right?

00:27:58.035 --> 00:28:02.790
"Rrr"-- all right-- that
wasn't really reassuring there.

00:28:02.790 --> 00:28:04.890
Establish an
operational process.

00:28:04.890 --> 00:28:07.240
New extensions are going
to be rolling in every day.

00:28:07.240 --> 00:28:09.073
So you're going to need
find a way to vet in

00:28:09.073 --> 00:28:11.880
and vet out, usually by
looking at the manifest

00:28:11.880 --> 00:28:15.270
and seeing what
permissions are present.

00:28:15.270 --> 00:28:17.520
Or since you have
your baseline of what

00:28:17.520 --> 00:28:19.350
permissions you
will or won't allow,

00:28:19.350 --> 00:28:21.450
you can just have the
user install them.

00:28:21.450 --> 00:28:23.700
And if it gets blocked, then
they can submit a ticket.

00:28:23.700 --> 00:28:25.950
And then you can take a
look at it at that point.

00:28:25.950 --> 00:28:29.160
And then review results,
survey your end users,

00:28:29.160 --> 00:28:31.950
roll up production in phases
to limit possible collateral

00:28:31.950 --> 00:28:33.360
damage.

00:28:33.360 --> 00:28:36.280
And here's how you can
get started right away.

00:28:36.280 --> 00:28:39.120
So sign up for Chrome
Browser Cloud Management.

00:28:39.120 --> 00:28:42.750
Take that first step to be
able to see what extensions are

00:28:42.750 --> 00:28:44.370
present on your end
users' machines,

00:28:44.370 --> 00:28:46.260
so you can start building
that master list.

00:28:46.260 --> 00:28:48.510
It's a really crucial
first step to make sure

00:28:48.510 --> 00:28:50.760
that you're going to be
effective with this management

00:28:50.760 --> 00:28:52.250
style.

00:28:52.250 --> 00:28:54.968
Test out the extension
management style

00:28:54.968 --> 00:28:56.260
that we're talking about today.

00:28:56.260 --> 00:29:00.223
So block by permission,
blocked by runtime host.

00:29:00.223 --> 00:29:02.640
And also, take a look at some
of the latest documentation.

00:29:02.640 --> 00:29:07.170
Like I said, we have a
white paper that's live now.

00:29:07.170 --> 00:29:08.490
It's actually linked.

00:29:08.490 --> 00:29:12.060
It's the second bullet
point, which is the extension

00:29:12.060 --> 00:29:13.410
management white paper.

00:29:13.410 --> 00:29:17.670
So I think that this will be
pushed to your guys' next app,

00:29:17.670 --> 00:29:20.250
via PDF, in a day or so.

